[Music]  hi I'm Justin Boz Derek I'm chef/owner  of speedy Romeo restaurant in Brooklyn  and Lower East Side so st. louis-style  pizza is one using that provel cheese  that the signifier that's what puts it  on the map that's what makes it unique  all the pizzas in st. Louis to use that  type of cheese they call it a mixture of  cheddar provolone and Swiss  there was a mayor in Saint Louis who  wanted to create a cheese to put them on  the map and so he wanted to cheese to be  the same consistency hot cold once you  cook it it get that caramelization on  top of them stays like creamy underneath  from there  cutting it in squares is another you  know stamp that makes it unique to st.  Louis as well and that Square party cut  is what they call so cooking process for  the st. Louie pizza is to shape the  dough we shape it into 12 inches and  then we put a thin layer of San Marzano  tomato sauce after the saucepan we have  six slices of thin hot sopressata  so a spicy almost pepperoni salami  flavor then from there we have  house-made Italian sausage so pour lots  of pecorino and Parmesan cheese toasted  fennel seed black pepper salt so little  dollops of that all over the pizza what  makes it the st. Louie pizza is the  provel cheese it's the consistency of  American cheese if you won't but it has  liquid smoke inside from there it gives  you that real rich umami flavor we put  more pecorino and Parmesan cheese chili  flake dried oregano and then from there  the pizza goes into the oven to coat our  oven ranges in the zone where we cook  the pizza from about 750 to 800 degrees  with it at that temp pizzas take about  90 seconds to two minutes to cook total  so it cooks you see the cheese do that  thing where caramel ice is on top stays  gooey underneath when it comes out then  we cut it into squares and then we  finish the pizza with pickled chilies  that acid and heat is great to balance  out that cream  cheesy flavor I grew up in Kansas City  my dad grew up in st. Louis once I knew  I was going to do a wood burning pizza  restaurant I selfishly brought the  provel in from st. Louis so that I could  eat it myself basically because I can't  get it anywhere around here now we're  bringing in like with both restaurants  close to 200 pounds a week of cheese  from st. Louis Brooklynites have come to  love the st. Louie pizza I was very  suspect when we first opened I didn't  think anyone would like it I thought  people would be stuck to their like New  York style pizza ways and not want to  try it but it's our second most popular  pizza you know once you have it it's  extremely craveable  [Music]

